# Paths carried even though .gitignore, .sesh-mover-ignore or the built-in
# excludes would drop them. Committed on purpose: it means the same thing on
# every clone and every machine.
#
# `.superpowers/` is the SDD ledger — per-task findings and the carried
# constraints ("never order across machines by pushedAt", "never floor
# mtimeMs", "never probe a binary with --help"), each with the measurement
# behind it. It is not in git, so losing it on a machine move means re-deriving
# all of it. GitHub issues carry what to do next; this carries what NOT to do
# and why we know, which is what stops a fixed defect being reintroduced.
#
# `docs/` WAS LISTED HERE and deliberately is not any more. This project's
# design specs moved into a separate private repo, and `docs/` is now a symlink
# into it — so they reach another machine by being cloned there, not by riding
# along in a bundle. The line would not work even if it stayed: `captureCarry`
# refuses a symlink on purpose, because a stat-based check follows it and would
# put the LINK TARGET's bytes in the payload. Removing it is bookkeeping, not a
# policy change.
#
# **If you do not have a docs repo, list your own `docs/` here.** An ordinary
# gitignored docs directory travelling beside the sessions is exactly what this
# file is for, and it is the case the design was built around.
#
# A pattern with a `/` anywhere in it is rooted at the project; a bare pattern
# matches that name at any depth. See README's include-list section.
.superpowers/
